{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>How goodly are your tents, etc.</b> The explanation is that among Israel there is one group that fixes set times for Torah, and another group that engages in it constantly. Corresponding to those who set times he said <b>How goodly are your tents, O Jacob</b> — like a tent that is not permanent; and corresponding to the constant group he said <b>your dwellings, O Israel</b> — for a dwelling (mishkan) is permanent. See Rashi's wording." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to correspond to two periods: (1) when they were in the wilderness, and HaShem was among them in a tent, as it is written (Exodus 26:7) ״וְעָשִׂיתָ וגו׳ לְאֹהֶל עַל הַמִּשְׁכָּן״, and corresponding to this he said ״How goodly are your tents.״ (2) when they were in the settled land, as it is written (I Kings 8:12) ״HaShem said to dwell in the arbefel,״ and corresponding to this he said ״your dwellings, O Israel.״ And he used plural language because there are gradations within the Tent of Meeting and the Mishkan, inside the curtain."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:39.045290299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}